WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If a division leaves something over, is the answer finished?

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:06.900 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are interpreting remainders.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:13.300 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will compare a situation where everyone needs a place
with a situation where the leftover amount can be shared.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.718 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The context decides what the remainder means.

00:00:17.368 --> 00:00:22.350 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to interpret remainders to answer
real-world division problems.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.757 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:24.977 --> 00:00:28.546 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can check that a remainder is smaller than the divisor.

00:00:28.766 --> 00:00:31.971 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can decide when a whole extra group is needed.

00:00:32.191 --> 00:00:35.523 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can share a remainder as a fraction when appropriate.

00:00:36.173 --> 00:00:39.025 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A remainder must be smaller than the divisor.

00:00:39.245 --> 00:00:43.254 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check a division using groups times group size plus the
remainder.

00:00:43.474 --> 00:00:48.564 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then decide whether to round up, keep whole groups, or share
the remainder as a fraction.

00:00:48.784 --> 00:00:54.201 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
In a small model, seventeen counters make three full groups
of five, with two left over.

00:00:54.851 --> 00:00:57.965 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Sixty-seven students need teams of up to eight.

00:00:58.185 --> 00:01:02.314 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Eight full teams hold sixty-four students, leaving three
students.

00:01:02.964 --> 00:01:06.258 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Everyone needs a team, so one more team is needed.

00:01:06.478 --> 00:01:10.721 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Nine teams have enough places, although the last team is not
full.

00:01:10.941 --> 00:01:13.646 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Eight teams would leave three students out.

00:01:14.296 --> 00:01:18.055 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Share twenty-three litres equally among four containers.

00:01:18.275 --> 00:01:23.122 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Five litres in each container uses twenty litres, leaving
three litres.

00:01:23.772 --> 00:01:26.357 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Share those three litres equally too.

00:01:26.577 --> 00:01:34.244 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each container gets three quarters of a litre more, so the
amount is five and three quarter litres, or five point seven
five litres.

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:38.646 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A remainder of three does not automatically mean point
three.

00:01:38.866 --> 00:01:43.899 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
In our litre problem, the three leftover litres are shared
between four containers.

00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:46.783 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each gets three quarters of a litre more.

00:01:47.003 --> 00:01:50.574 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The divisor and the story explain the fractional part.

00:01:51.224 --> 00:01:55.172 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Thirty-one students travel in cars with five student seats
each.

00:01:55.392 --> 00:01:57.156 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How many cars are needed?

00:02:01.156 --> 00:02:04.754 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Six cars hold thirty students, leaving one student.

00:02:04.974 --> 00:02:09.507 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A seventh car is needed, so the contextual answer is seven
cars.

00:02:10.157 --> 00:02:13.665 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Calculate and check the remainder, then return to the story.

00:02:13.885 --> 00:02:18.050 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
People need whole places; divisible quantities can be shared
further.

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:21.303 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain your answer using the correct unit.

00:02:21.523 --> 00:02:24.826 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a model and explain why your strategy works.
